Acceptance tests you must satisfy:
     1. {'type': 'behavior', 'restatement_span_id': 'detect-warn', 'description': "Feed the checker a code sample where a function dispatches on string comparisons ('pending', 'done') that match the values of an Enum defined right there in the same file. The checker should report exactly one warning, and its wording should be exactly the required message about stringly-typed dispatch.", 'proves': 'Confirms the core rule actually fires, fires exactly once (not zero, not duplicated), and uses the exact required warning wording when string dispatch shadows a same-module Enum.', 'when': {'interpreter': 'python3', 'script': 'scripts/check_stringly_dispatch.py', 'args': [], 'stdin': 'from enum import Enum\n\n\nclass Status(Enum):\n    PENDING = "pending"\n    DONE = "done"\n\n\ndef handle(status_str):\n    if status_str == "pending":\n        return "wait"\n    elif status_str == "done":\n        return "finish"\n'}, 'then': [{'observe': 'stdout_json', 'field': 'warning_count', 'op': 'eq', 'value': 1}, {'observe': 'stdout_json', 'field': 'first_message', 'op': 'eq', 'value': 'stringly-typed dispatch; dispatch on the Enum so exhaustiveness checking applies'}], 'max_runs': 1, 'timeout_seconds': 30, 'clause_id': 'c1'}
     2. {'type': 'behavior', 'restatement_span_id': 'boundary-optout', 'description': "Feed the checker the exact same code sample as above, except the function now carries the marker comment '# asp-fsm: boundary-parse' just above the dispatch. The checker should report zero warnings for this file.", 'proves': 'Confirms the boundary-parse marker comment fully suppresses the warning on a function that would otherwise trigger it, giving people a working escape hatch for legitimate boundary parsing.', 'when': {'interpreter': 'python3', 'script': 'scripts/check_stringly_dispatch.py', 'args': [], 'stdin': 'from enum import Enum\n\n\nclass Status(Enum):\n    PENDING = "pending"\n    DONE = "done"\n\n\ndef handle(status_str):\n    # asp-fsm: boundary-parse\n    if status_str == "pending":\n        return "wait"\n    elif status_str == "done":\n        return "finish"\n'}, 'then': [{'observe': 'stdout_json', 'field': 'warning_count', 'op': 'eq', 'value': 0}], 'max_runs': 1, 'timeout_seconds': 30, 'clause_id': 'c2'}
     3. {'type': 'behavior', 'restatement_span_id': 'no-enum-silent', 'description': "Feed the checker a code sample that dispatches on the same string literals ('pending', 'done') but where no Enum exists anywhere in the file. The checker should report zero warnings, since there is no Enum being shadowed.", 'proves': 'Confirms the rule does not false-positive on ordinary string dispatch that has no corresponding same-module Enum to route on instead.', 'when': {'interpreter': 'python3', 'script': 'scripts/check_stringly_dispatch.py', 'args': [], 'stdin': 'def handle(kind):\n    if kind == "pending":\n        return "wait"\n    elif kind == "done":\n        return "finish"\n'}, 'then': [{'observe': 'stdout_json', 'field': 'warning_count', 'op': 'eq', 'value': 0}], 'max_runs': 1, 'timeout_seconds': 30, 'clause_id': 'c3'}

When the task is done, complete it through the typed-handoff contract (do NOT open a pull request — the exophial reducer integrates your branch itself once the handoff is valid):

1. Commit ALL your work to this branch (the reducer merges from it).
2. Write a YAML handoff to .pebbles/handoffs/asp-5be.yaml that validates against the
   exophial done.schema.json. For a successful task it MUST contain:
     - status: completed
     - pebble_id: asp-5be
     - summary: one paragraph on what you did and how you verified it
     - cost: your token spend for this task (e.g. "~40k tokens")
     - commits: the list of commit SHAs you made on this branch. Write
       each SHA as a QUOTED string (e.g. "6983640"), never a bare
       scalar — YAML parses an all-digit bare SHA as an integer, which
       fails schema validation.
     - verify_transcript: a mapping of verb label -> stdout tail for
       every verify command you ran (e.g. pytest, mypy, ruff)
     - EXACTLY ONE of e2e_gate OR e2e_exempt (never both, never
       neither):
         * If a REAL no-mock outer-loop test proves this change, use
           e2e_gate with ALL these fields and no others: spec, command, result, ran_at_sha, mocks_in_path.
           result MUST be the string "passed"; mocks_in_path MUST be
           the boolean false; ran_at_sha is the branch commit you ran
           the test at, written as a QUOTED string (e.g. "6983640"),
           never a bare scalar — YAML parses an all-digit bare SHA as
           an integer, which fails schema validation; spec is the
           test's repo-relative path; command is the exact command you
           ran.
         * Otherwise (no real outer-loop test — e.g. a trivial,
           infrastructure, docs, or refactor change), use e2e_exempt
           with {category, reason}. category MUST be exactly one of:
           docs, refactor, infrastructure, pebble-filing, hygiene, outer-loop-undrivable. reason must be at least 12 characters.
   If you could NOT finish, instead write status: blocked (with
   blocker + next_action) or status: failed (with failure_diagnosis);
   pebble_id, summary, and cost are always required.

The gate watches .pebbles/handoffs/asp-5be.yaml; if you do not write it, the task times out and nothing merges.
